# G086 — Quick Launcher

*v2. See `quick-launcher.md` for prose: frecency as one scalar that
replaces frequency-vs-recency tradeoff, two-layer ranking (text match
primary, frecency bonus for ties), usage state as round-trip file,
empty query surfaces everything by frecency.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Vault Launcher

[vault-launcher
    IN ORDER TO
        surface the entry a user wants from a prefix they type
        while recording usage so tomorrow's ranking is better
        and persisting that usage across sessions as a text file

    @state    <- [vault[read-string {path: ".vault/launcher-state.txt"}]]
    @launcher <- [launcher[from-text {text: @state}]]

    sync @user-events {
        type @query ->
            @matches <- [launcher[query
                                    {launcher: @launcher,
                                     text: @query,
                                     now: [clock[now]]}]]
            [ui[render-hits {matches: @matches}]]

        choose @entry-id ->
            @entry <- [launcher[get
                                  {launcher: @launcher, id: @entry-id}]]
                || -> #unknown-entry @entry-id
            [shell[run {command: @entry:action}]]
            [launcher[record-launch
                        {launcher: @launcher,
                         id: @entry-id, now: [clock[now]]}]]
            [vault[save
                    {path: ".vault/launcher-state.txt",
                     content: [launcher[to-text {launcher: @launcher}]]}]]
    }
]

---

## Verb Surface

[launcher
    new
    add              {launcher, entry}
    get              {launcher, id}                ;; || not-found
    query            {launcher, text, now}          ;; -> [Match]
    record-launch    {launcher, id, now}            ;; || unknown-entry
    to-text          {launcher}                     ;; -> String
    from-text        {text}                         ;; || parse-error
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: two-layer ranking expressed as `where` tiers

[launcher[query {launcher: @l, text: @q, now: @now}]
    @l:entries
    IN ORDER TO
        return matches whose visibility is decided by text match
        and whose order within the visible set is decided by frecency

    where text-match(@q, @entry) is greater than zero
        or @q is empty
    ordered by
        text-match descending,
        frecency(count, last, @now) descending
]

Text match is strictly primary: a perfect match (1.0) beats any
frecency bonus (weight 0.1 × log-compressed count ≤ 0.7). Empty
query assigns every entry a constant text match so frecency fully
determines order — the "show me everything" state.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: state persists `where` count and last survive

[launcher[to-text {launcher: @l}]
    IN ORDER TO
        serialise the entry roster AND the accumulated usage state
        where every entry's count and last-launched-ms are preserved
        so that from-text ∘ to-text is the identity on launchers
]

G085 committed to round-trip for static configuration. G086 extends
the contract to mutable state — if the file loses launch counts
between sessions, the launcher re-learns the user every day.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @l <- [launcher[new]]
    [launcher[add {launcher: @l,
                    entry: {id: 1, name: "Firefox", action: "firefox",
                             keywords: ["browser", "web"]}}]]
    [launcher[add {launcher: @l,
                    entry: {id: 2, name: "Files", action: "nautilus",
                             keywords: ["folder"]}}]]
    [launcher[add {launcher: @l,
                    entry: {id: 3, name: "Terminal", action: "kitty",
                             keywords: ["shell", "cli"]}}]]
    [launcher[add {launcher: @l,
                    entry: {id: 4, name: "File Manager", action: "thunar",
                             keywords: ["folder"]}}]]

    -> [launcher[query {launcher: @l, text: "file", now: 0}]]
    ;; -> [{id:2, 0.8}, {id:4, 0.8}]  — tied prefix match, no frecency yet

    [launcher[record-launch {launcher: @l, id: 2, now: 1000000}]]
    [launcher[record-launch {launcher: @l, id: 2, now: 1000000}]]
    [launcher[record-launch {launcher: @l, id: 2, now: 1000000}]]

    -> [launcher[query {launcher: @l, text: "file", now: 2000000}]]
    ;; -> [{id:2, ~0.94}, {id:4, 0.8}]  — frecency broke the tie

    -> [launcher[query {launcher: @l, text: "fire", now: 1000000}]]
    ;; -> [{id:1, 0.8}]  — Firefox, never launched, still wins on text match

    -> [launcher[from-text
                   {text: [launcher[to-text {launcher: @l}]]}]]
       is equal to @l                               ;; -> true
]
